          "type": "intro",
          "title": "From Survival to Transcendence",
          "content": "For thousands of years, humanity wrestled with three ancient enemies: famine, plague, and war. Harari opens Homo Deus by arguing that in the 21st century, these problems have been transformed from incomprehensible forces of nature into manageable challenges. Today, more people die from eating too much than too little, from old age than from infectious disease, and from suicide than from war. This unprecedented success raises a startling question: what will humanity do next? With the old agenda largely resolved, Harari predicts we will set our sights on three new projects worthy of gods themselves: immortality, bliss, and divinity. This lesson introduces the framework that drives the entire book.",
